    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "Why does borrowing more raise the equity returns in an LBO? Show it with a company bought for $1,000M and sold a year later for $1,100M.",
    a: "Because the equity keeps every dollar of gain above the debt's fixed cost while putting in less money. All equity, the $100M gain is a 10% return. Borrow $600M at 7% and the equity puts in $400M, pays $42M of interest and keeps $58M: 14.5%. It works only while the business earns more than the debt costs.",
    why: "Lenders get a fixed return and the equity gets everything else. When the assets earn more than the debt costs, the spread on every borrowed dollar accrues to a smaller equity base, so the return rises with leverage. The same arithmetic runs in reverse: sell for $950M and the all-equity return is −5%, the levered one −23%. A real LBO adds two effects: interest is tax-deductible, and free cash flow repays debt each year, so the equity's share of the business grows.",
