    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "Why do private equity firms like businesses with recurring revenue?",
    a: "Because predictable revenue makes cash flow predictable, and predictable cash flow can carry more debt on better terms. Recurring revenue also cushions a downturn, makes the plan easier to underwrite and usually earns a higher exit multiple. Each of those raises the sponsor's return or lowers its risk.",
    why: "An LBO's main risk is failing to service its debt in a bad year. With subscriptions, long contracts or repeat purchases, most of next year's revenue is known today, so EBITDA is less likely to drop sharply. Lenders will lend more turns against it; some even lend against recurring revenue before a company is profitable. Buyers at exit pay more for durable revenue, so the multiple holds up too. What matters is retention: recurring revenue with high churn is less recurring than it looks.",
