    topic: "Term loan A vs term loan B",
    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What's the difference between a term loan A and a term loan B?",
    a: "A term loan A is held by banks, amortizes steadily over about five or six years and carries maintenance covenants and a lower spread. A term loan B is sold to institutional investors, largely CLOs: it repays about 1% a year with the rest due at maturity, often seven years out, pays a higher spread and is often covenant-lite.",
    why: "Each loan fits its lender. Banks want their money back steadily, with regular covenant tests as an early warning. Institutional investors, such as CLOs that pool leveraged loans into rated securities, want to stay invested and earn a spread, so they accept a bullet maturity for a higher rate. Sponsors usually prefer TLBs: little required amortization leaves cash free for growth or voluntary paydown, and the lack of maintenance covenants gives room in a downturn. Both are floating rate, senior secured and prepayable at or near par.",
    trap: "Many large LBOs have no TLA at all: the senior debt is a revolver plus a TLB, sometimes alongside bonds. TLAs are more common in smaller, bank-led deals, often held by the same banks that provide the revolver."
