    q: "When would a sponsor put high-yield bonds into an LBO's capital structure instead of borrowing more through term loans?",
    a: "When the deal needs more debt than the loan market will take, and the sponsor expects a hold long enough to live with call protection. Bonds add a long-dated, fixed-rate layer, often unsecured behind the loans, with only incurrence covenants. For a quick flip or a plan to repay early, loans are better, since they're prepayable at or near par.",
    why: "The choice turns on size, horizon and flexibility. Bond issues carry high fixed costs and must be large enough to trade, so they appear mostly in bigger buyouts. Their fixed rate protects the company if rates rise, and with no amortization or maintenance covenants there's room in a downturn. The cost is rigidity: a non-call period, often a few years, then premiums that step down to par. So sponsors match the call schedule to the exit plan, since a sale or refinancing inside the non-call period triggers a make-whole payment that comes out of the equity's proceeds.",
    trap: "The follow-up: what if rates fall after the deal? The loans' interest falls with the base rate, but the bonds keep their fixed coupon until they can be called, so the company can't capture the savings without paying a premium."
