    why: "Debt capacity comes from cash flow, not from the price, and the lenders' limit is a ceiling, not a target. More debt raises the base-case IRR, but each extra turn costs more, tightens covenant headroom and raises the chance of losing the equity in a bad year. So sponsors size debt on the downside case: can the company still cover interest, stay inside its covenants and fund itself from the revolver? The answer depends on how volatile EBITDA is and how much of it turns into cash.",
    example: "EBITDA [[$100M]]; lenders offer up to [[6.0x]] at [[8%]]. At 6.0x, interest is $48M: 2.1x coverage. If EBITDA falls [[25%]] to $75M, coverage drops to 1.6x, and after [[$20M]] of capex only $7M is left before taxes and repayment. At [[5.0x]], interest is $40M, downside coverage is 1.9x and $15M is left.",
    trap: "Lenders size on adjusted EBITDA, which may include add-backs and cost savings not yet achieved. Check leverage on EBITDA you actually believe, or the true multiple is higher than the headline."
