    a: "The revolver covers it. The model keeps a minimum cash balance, and if beginning cash plus free cash flow, less mandatory repayments, would fall below it, the revolver draws the shortfall. When cash later exceeds the minimum, the revolver is repaid first, before any optional prepayment of the term loans.",
    why: "Mandatory amortization is contractual, and the business can't run its cash to zero, so the revolver acts as the model's plug. It shows how much short-term borrowing the plan needs and whether the facility is big enough, a key downside test. Repaying it first is the usual convention: it can be drawn again if needed, so paying it down costs no flexibility, while a repaid term loan is gone. The minimum cash level is a judgment, often a few percent of revenue or a set dollar amount.",
