    topic: "Interest circularity",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "Why does an LBO model usually have a circular reference, and how do you handle it?",
    a: "Because interest depends on the average debt balance, the balance depends on the cash swept and the sweep depends on free cash flow, which is after interest. You can let Excel iterate, with a circuit-breaker switch to break the loop if it errors, or calculate interest on beginning balances, which removes the circularity at a small cost in accuracy.",
    why: "Average-balance interest is more accurate when debt falls during the year, but it forces Excel to solve the loop by iteration. If an error enters, it can feed on itself and stick, so models include a toggle that temporarily sets interest to beginning balances or zero, breaking the loop so the error clears. Interest income on cash creates the same loop. Paper LBOs and many quick models use beginning balances, which slightly overstates interest when debt is falling. Some banks ban circularity and use a copy-paste macro instead.",
    example: "Term loan [[$500M]] at [[8%]]. Beginning-balance interest is $40.0M. If [[$50M]] is swept during the year, average-balance interest is 8% × $475M = $38.0M. That $2.0M saving raises free cash flow and the sweep, which moves the average again, until the numbers settle.",
    trap: "Iteration doesn't fix a broken model. If the loop won't converge, turn the switch off, find the error and turn it back on; a sweep formula that can exceed the cash available is a common culprit."
