    why: "Exit equity is enterprise value minus debt plus cash, so a dollar used to repay debt and a dollar left in the bank raise it equally. The paydown bucket really measures cash the business produced after interest, taxes and capex. What repayment adds is lower risk and interest saved at the loan's rate. That's also why a dividend recap can raise IRR: returning cash early beats repaying debt when the IRR the sponsor earns exceeds the debt's after-tax cost.",
    example: "Two identical companies each generate [[$250M]] of free cash flow over the hold, starting with [[$600M]] of debt and exiting at [[$1,300M]] of enterprise value. One repays debt to $350M; the other keeps the cash. Exit equity is $950M either way: $1,300M − $350M, or $1,300M − $600M + $250M.",
    trap: "A more honest label for the bucket is cash generation or deleveraging. The follow-up: then why repay debt at all? Credit agreements require part of it, and it cuts interest and risk."
