    q: "Why does a sponsor line up committed financing before signing an LBO, and what are bridge loans and flex?",
    a: "Because the seller wants certainty the deal will close, so sponsor deals rarely have a financing condition. Banks sign commitment letters to fund the loans and a bridge loan in case the bonds can't be sold by closing. Flex lets the banks change pricing or structure within agreed limits if investors won't buy the debt on the original terms.",
    why: "If financing falls through, the sponsor usually owes the seller a reverse termination fee, so it needs its lenders bound as tightly as it is. Banks underwrite the debt and then sell it to investors. The bridge is expensive by design, with rates that step up over time, so the company issues the bonds to replace it. Flex shifts the market risk between signing and syndication onto the borrower, which is why the sponsor models the flexed terms as well as the base case.",
